course: euphemisms
lesson: 37
title: Soft words for hard things: stupidity and madness and rudeness and swearing
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Many jokey phrases for "stupid" or "mad" describe something with a piece missing: a deck short of one card, a lift that stops before the top floor, a load short of two bricks.
explain: The listener has to finish the picture: if a part is missing, the person's mind is missing a part too. That is why these phrases are funny but also unkind.
explain: Swearing has soft words too. Long ago it was thought wrong to say God's or Jesus's name in anger, so people bent the words: God became "gosh" and "golly", Jesus became "gee" and "jeez", Christ became "crikey" and "cripes".
explain: So when a grandmother drops a cup and cries "Gosh darn it!", she is saying a much stronger curse in a polite shape that nobody minds.
fact: "Bats in the belfry" means mad; it is an old-fashioned joke, as if bats were flapping about in the bell tower of one's head.
fact: "The elevator doesn't go all the way to the top" means a person is stupid or a bit mad; it is an American joke.
fact: "Lost the plot" means gone mad or started behaving in a wild, senseless way; it is British slang.
fact: "A bit touched" means slightly mad; it is old-fashioned and gentle, often heard in Ireland.
fact: "Out to lunch" means mad, or not paying attention at all; it is American slang.
fact: "One card short of a full deck" means stupid or a bit mad; it is a humorous phrase.
fact: "Not the sharpest knife in the drawer" means not very clever; it is a humorous phrase.
fact: "Two bricks shy of a load" means stupid or a bit mad; it is American and humorous.
fact: "Gosh" is a mild word used instead of "God" when surprised.
fact: "Golly" is an old-fashioned mild word used instead of "God".
fact: "Gee" is a mild word used instead of "Jesus"; it is mostly American.
fact: "Jeez" is a mild word used instead of "Jesus", said in surprise or annoyance.
fact: "Jeepers" is an old-fashioned American mild word used instead of "Jesus".
fact: "Crikey" is a mild word used instead of "Christ"; it is British and Australian.
fact: "Cripes" is an old-fashioned mild word used instead of "Christ".
fact: "For crying out loud" is a mild way of saying "for Christ's sake", showing annoyance.
fact: "For Pete's sake" is a mild way of saying "for God's sake", showing annoyance.
fact: "Gosh darn it" is an American polite form of the curse "God damn it".
fact: "Darn" is a mild word used instead of the swear word "damn".
fact: "Dang" is a mild word used instead of "damn"; it is American.
exercise: What does it mean if someone has "bats in the belfry"? | He is mad | He keeps pets in a tower | He works in a church | He is scared of the dark
exercise: What does "the elevator doesn't go all the way to the top" mean about a person? | He is stupid or a bit mad | He lives on a low floor | He is afraid of heights | He is very short
exercise: What does "she's lost the plot" mean? | She has started behaving wildly or madly | She has lost the story she was writing | She has lost her garden plot | She has forgotten a film's ending
exercise: An Irish granny says the old neighbour is "a bit touched". What does she mean? | He is slightly mad | He is a bit hurt | He is very kind | He is a bit sunburnt
exercise: What does "he's out to lunch" usually mean when someone acts strangely? | He is mad or not paying attention | He is eating in a café | He has left work for a break | He is very hungry
exercise: What does "one card short of a full deck" mean? | Stupid or a bit mad | Bad at card games | Missing a birthday card | Short of money
exercise: What does "not the sharpest knife in the drawer" mean? | Not very clever | Not very dangerous | Not good at cooking | Not very tidy
exercise: What does "two bricks shy of a load" mean? | Stupid or a bit mad | Very shy and quiet | Bad at building walls | Very tired from work
exercise: What word does "gosh" stand in for? | God | Good | Ghost | Gas
exercise: What does "golly" stand in for? | God | Gold | Jolly | Goal
exercise: An American says "Gee, that's big!" What word is "gee" a soft form of? | Jesus | Great | Giant | Geese
exercise: "Jeez, that was close!" What word is "jeez" a soft form of? | Jesus | Cheese | Geese | Jeans
exercise: What is "jeepers" a soft form of? | Jesus | Jeeps | Peepers | Creepers
exercise: An Australian says "Crikey!" at a big crocodile. What word is "crikey" a soft form of? | Christ | Crocodile | Cricket | Crazy
exercise: What is "cripes" a mild stand-in for? | Christ | Crisps | Grapes | Cries
exercise: What does "for crying out loud" really show? | Annoyance, as a soft "for Christ's sake" | That someone is crying very loudly in pain | That someone must shout | That a baby is awake
exercise: Who is Pete in "for Pete's sake"? | Nobody; it stands in for "for God's sake" | A saint called Peter who looked after the poor | The speaker's brother | A famous old king
exercise: What does "gosh darn it" stand in for? | God damn it | Goodness, darn my socks | Gosh, I am tired | Go and do it
exercise: What swear word is "darn" a soft form of? | Damn | Dark | Darling | Dawn
exercise: "Dang, I missed the bus." What is "dang" a soft form of? | Damn | Danger | Dong | Dance
test: News: "The striker missed an open goal and the commentator cried, 'Crikey!'" What did the commentator show? | Surprise, with a soft word for Christ | That the striker is called Crikey | That he wanted a cricket game | That the goal was a good one
test: "ugh my bro is so not the sharpest knife in the drawer, he put the cat food in the fridge AND the milk in the cupboard." What does the speaker think of the brother? | He is not very clever | He is good at cooking | He keeps knives in the drawer | He is very tidy
test: Grandpa: "That fellow's got bats in the belfry, he paints his car every Tuesday." What does Grandpa think? | The man is a bit mad | The man keeps bats | The man is a bell ringer | The man is a good painter
test: Ben says "gosh" is a rude swear word and you'll get a detention for it. right? | No, it is a mild polite stand-in for "God" | Yes, it is the worst swear word there is | Yes, but only in the United States | No, it is a word for a kind of bird
test: "for crying out loud, close the flipping door!" How does the speaker feel? | Annoyed | Sad and crying | Scared | Very happy
test: A coach shouts, "Where's your head? You're out to lunch today!" What does he mean? | You are not paying attention | You may go and eat now | You missed lunch today | You should eat more
test: Neighbour gossip: "After the lottery win he totally lost the plot, bought six boats." What happened? | He started behaving wildly and senselessly | He forgot the whole story of a film he watched | He lost his piece of land | He won a garden contest
test: "dang it, i dropped my phone, no wait, it's fine." What is "dang" here? | A mild curse word instead of "damn" | A word for a phone ringing | A sound a phone makes when it breaks | A name for the speaker's dog
test: Which of these is NOT about being stupid or mad? | Jeepers | Two bricks shy of a load | One card short of a full deck | The elevator doesn't go to the top
test: Gran: "Darn! I've burnt the pie." Why does she say "darn" and not a stronger word? | It is a polite form of the curse "damn" | Because she is mending a sock with a needle | Because "darn" means burnt | Because the pie was dark
test: Why do phrases like "one card short of a full deck" mean someone is stupid? | They picture something missing, like a part of the mind | Because stupid people lose cards | Because people who play card games all day become stupid | Because a full deck is very heavy
test: "Gee whiz, your dog's huge!" What is "gee" a soft form of? | Jesus | Giant | Good | Grow
exam: Identify the word for which the exclamation "crikey" constitutes a softened substitute. | Christ | Cricket | Crocodile | Crisis
exam: Ascertain the meaning most accurately conveyed by the statement that an individual is "not the sharpest knife in the drawer". | The individual is of limited intelligence | The individual is of a hostile disposition | The individual is lacking in culinary skill | The individual is of an untidy habit
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the expression "for Pete's sake". | It is a mild substitute for "for God's sake" | It refers to a particular saint named Peter | It is a formal expression of gratitude | It is the strongest of all English oaths
exam: Where it is stated that a person "has lost the plot", determine the condition so described. | The person has begun to behave in an irrational manner | The person has mislaid a parcel of land | The person has forgotten the narrative of a novel | The person has been deprived of an allotment
